      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.cbc.ca/news/canada/toronto/world-cup-tickets-ontario-resale-regulations-9.7023518?cmp=rss</guid>
      <description><![CDATA[<img src='https://i.cbc.ca/ais/1.7104790,1748015035000/full/max/0/default.jpg?im=Crop%2Crect%3D%28500%2C0%2C5673%2C3191%29%3BResize%3D%28620%29' alt='People holding a large soccer banner in the middle of an empty soccer field' width='620' height='349' title='Toronto city councillors will have voted to clamp down on spending associated with the games as the city and province fight over funding. BMO Field, seen here, will be the site of the games and must be expanded to accommodate the event.'/><p>With tickets to next year's six World Cup games in Toronto selling out as quickly as they're released, the only option for most soccer fans is the resale market. But a 2019 decision by the Ontario government has allowed tickets to those matches, and other major events, to go sky high.</p>]]></description>
